course: dictionary
lesson: 3106
title: Either, or, and both
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Either means one or the other of two things. «You can have either an apple or a pear» means you pick one of the two, not both. Either and or work as a pair to show a choice.
explain: Either can also mean each of two. «There are trees on either side of the road» means one tree on the left and one on the right. To check, swap in each: the sentence still works with each in its place.
explain: After not, either means also. «It is not naughtiness either» means it is not naughtiness, and it is not that either. Swap in also: if the sentence still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
fact: Either means one or the other of two things, as in «either an apple or a pear».
fact: Either can mean each of two: «either costs $5» means each one costs $5.
fact: «Either way» means whichever happens.
fact: After «not», either means also, as in «she could not dance either».
fact: Either can be a determiner, a pronoun, an adverb or a conjunction, so check the sentence to see which job it does.
exercise: «You can have either an apple or a pear.» How many pieces of fruit can you take? | None at all | *Just one | Both of them | Five
exercise: «There are trees on either side of the road.» What does either mean here? | *Each of two | Not at all | Only the left one | Neither of two
exercise: Find and copy the word that means also in «Ruby laughed so much she could not dance either.» | much | laughed | *either | could
test: «Either way, we will get there.» What does either way mean? | Left and right together | Neither way is possible | Only by the main road | *Whichever happens
test: «Either costs $5.» What does this mean? | The two together cost $5 | Neither costs anything | Only the first costs $5 | *Each one costs $5
exam: Lee says, «It is not naughtiness either.» Which meaning of either fits best? | Each of two | Whichever happens | One or the other of two | *Also
